{"idea_id": "idea_cudagraph_fa3_walltime", "version": 1, "summary": "The 5-min frame is throughput-bound. Its frozen baseline inherited COMPILE_MODE=max-autotune-no-cudagraphs from the step-budgeted config, where cudagraphs were dropped to free ~25GB for bigger n-gram tables and step-time was explicitly irrelevant. In a time-budgeted frame that tradeoff flips: re-enabling cudagraph capture (max-autotune) should complete more steps in 300s at identical per-step math, lowering val_bpb -- unless the ~25GB pool OOMs.", "experimental_plan": "Paired A/B on one seed (stage-1 discovery). Control = frozen baseline (fa3, max-autotune-no-cudagraphs). Treatment = same, COMPILE_MODE=max-autotune (int_fa3_cudagraphs). Same frame budget (STOP_MODE=time, 300s), same data order. Read step_time_ms, steps-in-300s, and endpoint val_bpb. Pilot proves it runs (no OOM), is numerically consistent at matched steps, and gains throughput; only then escalate to 3 pairs.", "direction": "systems_kernel", "subsystem": "compile_cudagraphs", "parent_idea_ids": [], "scores": {"interestingness": {"score": 6, "rationale": "Clean frame-appropriateness insight -- a config chosen for a step-budgeted frame is likely wrong for a time-budgeted one -- aimed straight at the throughput bottleneck. Docked because it is a config lever, not a deep algorithmic idea."}, "novelty": {"score": 3, "rationale": "Low as literature novelty: max-autotune is a standard, known-working compile mode. Novel only as an untested frame-specific configuration; the idea archive is empty so it does not duplicate any prior idea."}, "feasibility": {"score": 9, "rationale": "One env var; a default compile mode that already reproduces the baseline. Only risk is the ~25GB cudagraph pool OOMing. Cheap 1-pair pilot."}}, "novelty_check": {"provider": "combined", "status": "passed", "query_rounds": [{"query": "CUDA graph capture kernel launch overhead language model training throughput torch.compile max-autotune", "result_paper_ids": ["pap_flash_attention_3"], "assessment": "Closest archived paper is FlashAttention-3, which optimizes work inside the attention kernel -- orthogonal to eliminating per-step CPU launch overhead via graph capture. No archived work targets this lever in a time-budgeted frame."}], "closest_paper_ids": ["pap_flash_attention_3"], "evidence_ids": ["evd_lit_cudagraph_launch_overhead"], "max_semantic_similarity": 0.28, "discard_threshold": 0.5, "assessment": "This is a systems/config lever, not a literature-novel algorithm; framed honestly, its value is empirical (does the cudagraph throughput gain beat its ~25GB VRAM cost in THIS frame?), not novelty. The archive is empty (novel by construction) and the closest external work, FlashAttention-3, is orthogonal (intra-kernel, not whole-step graph capture), so lexical/semantic similarity is low (0.28 < 0.5 threshold)."}, "hypothesis_id": "hyp_cudagraph_fa3_walltime", "title": "Re-enable CUDA-graph capture for the time-budgeted 5-min frame", "status": "selected", "created_at": "2026-07-25T09:00:00-07:00", "notes": "Direction chosen with the user (2026-07-25); first funnel of the 5-min campaign.", "fingerprint": "03e8af447176b6fa"}
{"idea_id": "idea_ngram_sparse_grad_walltime", "version": 1, "summary": "The 5-min baseline carries n-gram VE tables at 64x and pays a dense ~5.6 GB gradient + full optimizer state every step. In a throughput-bound frame that memory traffic is dead weight: NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD (touched-row writes) + NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE ((rows,1) state) cut it at near-identical numerics, which should buy more steps in 300s -- unless the custom-op overhead cancels the saving at 64x or grad_accum!=1 forbids it.", "experimental_plan": "Paired A/B on one seed (stage-1 discovery). Control = frozen baseline (dense n-gram grad, full state). Treatment = NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD=1 + NGRAM_STATE_ROWWISE=1. Same frame budget, same data order. Read step_time_ms, steps-in-300s, and endpoint val_bpb; confirm matched-step quality within rounding. Escalate only if it runs, is numerically consistent, and gains throughput.", "direction": "memory_retrieval", "subsystem": "ngram_sparse_grad", "parent_idea_ids": [], "scores": {"interestingness": {"score": 5, "rationale": "Reuses existing memory-efficiency machinery for a throughput end in a frame it was not built for; modest but genuinely uncertain at 64x."}, "novelty": {"score": 3, "rationale": "Low literature novelty -- the knobs exist and are documented; novel only as a throughput (not capacity) use in the time-budgeted frame. Distinct subsystem from the CUDA-graph idea so the funnels do not duplicate."}, "feasibility": {"score": 8, "rationale": "Two existing env vars, no new code. Main risk is grad_accum!=1 (fast clean failure) or the 64x saving being too small to matter."}}, "novelty_check": {"provider": "combined", "status": "passed", "query_rounds": [{"query": "sparse gradient row-wise optimizer state memory traffic training throughput embedding tables", "result_paper_ids": ["pap_recent_optim_galore"], "assessment": "Closest archived paper is GaLore (memory-efficient optimizer state via low-rank projection). Its method differs from sparse row-writes for gather-only n-gram tables; it supports only the premise that gradient-memory footprint is a lever."}], "closest_paper_ids": ["pap_recent_optim_galore"], "evidence_ids": ["evd_lit_ngram_sparse_grad_throughput"], "max_semantic_similarity": 0.3, "discard_threshold": 0.5, "assessment": "A systems/memory lever repurposed for throughput, not a literature-novel algorithm. The archive is empty of ideas so it is novel by construction; the closest external work (GaLore) shares only the premise (memory footprint is reducible), not the sparse-row mechanism, so similarity is low (0.3 < 0.5). Distinct subsystem (memory_retrieval) from the CUDA-graph funnel (systems_kernel)."}, "hypothesis_id": "hyp_ngram_sparse_grad_walltime", "title": "Memory-efficient n-gram gradient as a 5-min throughput lever", "status": "selected", "created_at": "2026-07-25T09:40:00-07:00", "notes": "Second parallel funnel of the 5-min campaign (2026-07-25); distinct subsystem from the CUDA-graph idea.", "fingerprint": "91c0d72ea9160544"}
